@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — the fifth protocol opened against a generalization, and it is
# opened against the head of the queue
#
# SPEC §16 makes the queue for stage three derivable rather than written: the
# generalizations on which no pm:Testing points, ordered by the number of
# attested claims behind them. Run against the holding on 2026-09-21, the head
# of that queue is the claim examined here, with eight predecessors and a ninth
# arriving in the same run. It had never been examined, and it is the oldest
# unexamined statement in the folder.
#
# The claim has two clauses and they are not equally carried, which the node
# itself says. The first is about how a calendar-bound condition is stated: as
# a date, and not as a state of the operator or of the person operated on. The
# second is about where such a transmission puts the failure when it states one
# for acting outside the window: in the time, and not in either party. Two
# questions therefore had to be put separately, which is the procedural lesson
# of the protocol before this one.
#
# The first question returned eight further confirming cases and no exception.
# The second returned almost nothing to confirm and one transmission that says
# a great deal about where failure lies while saying nothing about the window —
# and that case is the finding. It does not refute the clause as worded. It
# shows what the clause as worded is worth.
#

pmp:CalendarFailureLocationExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether a calendar-bound transmission puts the failure in the time"@en, "Ob eine kalendergebundene Überlieferung das Misslingen in die Zeit legt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that transmissions binding an operation to a calendar state the condition as a date rather than as a state of a party, and that where such a transmission states a consequence for acting outside the window it locates the failure in the time. The population is every node in the compiled holding that binds an operation, or the handling of the material an operation needs, to a date, an hour, a month, a lunar phase, a season or a constellation. It was drawn by a whole-text scan of traditions/, convergences/ and originations/ for the vocabulary of dates, and every candidate was read at its place and classified on two axes: whether the condition is a date or a state of a party, and whether the transmission states any consequence for missing it."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:AdmissibilityBoundToTheCalendar ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "For the first clause: a transmission that binds an operation to a calendar and states the condition as a state of the operator or of the person operated on rather than as a date. Not met; nothing in the population does this, and the two cases that come closest are releases from the calendar rather than statements of it. For the second clause: a transmission that states a consequence for acting outside its own window and locates that consequence in a party rather than in the time. Not met either, and the reason is the finding of the protocol — almost no transmission in the population states a consequence for acting outside the window at all."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Two searches over the compiled holding, run as two questions. The first drew every node whose text carries the vocabulary of dates — day, hour, lunar day, lunation, new and full moon, fortnight, named weekday, season, month, solstice, equinox, constellation, planetary, auspicious and unlucky day, stem, calendar, zodiac — and classified each by whether the condition it states is a date or a state of a party; some forty nodes were drawn and the fifteen that bind an operation were read at their places. The second asked the opposite question of the same population: which of them says anything at all about what follows from missing the window, and where it puts the failure. That is where the opposition came from. Not searched: anything outside this holding. The claim is about the wording of transmissions and the holding carries some ninety corpora, so the cheap search is the one that was run; a transmission outside it that binds an operation to a calendar and blames the operator for a session held inside the window would tell against the second clause exactly as the case found here does."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-21" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-21" ;
    skos:note "Eight confirming cases for the first clause beyond the predecessors, all found in the first search and all read at their places. The seventeenth chapter of the Baopuzi lays three calendars over one another for the entering of the mountains — the third and ninth months when the mountain opens, each of the five peaks closed in the years and months of its own phase, then a list of forbidden days by month and a third list of six days in a long month and five in a short one. The handbook of European folk magic prints a table of some thirty-two unlucky days of the year. The science of letters prescribes seven days of the lunation for its orations. The Key of Solomon fixes four of the seven capacities of its first book to a month, a planetary day and hour, a date in June, a phase of the moon and a span between midnight and daybreak. The rubric of the hundred and fortieth chapter of the Book of the Dead fixes its offering to the last day of the second month of the season pert, at the moment the sun shows himself. And the rasayana lesson of the Caraka binds six separate operations to a window. In every one of them the condition is a date and in none of them is it a state of a party."@en ;
    skos:note "For the second clause the harvest is thin, and the thinness is the first half of the finding. Of the fifteen transmissions in the population, four state a consequence for acting outside the window and eleven state none. The four are the ones the claim was built on: the hours of dead qi in which the circulation of the qi is of no benefit, the going wrong of the year and the season that makes a burial adverse however correct the rite, the prohibited days of the Zhen'gao whose harm is done before the person notices it, and the mbattal days of the Mandaean reckoning. The chapter on entering the mountains joins them and is the eighth witness for that clause: whoever enters on such a day will assuredly be tested by the spirit of the mountain, will not obtain what he sought and will not accomplish what he set out to do, and the ordinary man who enters on those days meets with harm and falls in with tigers and wolves. The failure is located in the day."@en ;
    skos:note "The deviation, and it is a case the claim's two columns have no room for. The sixty-fourth chapter of the Suwen indexes the depth of needling by the season and then names, for each season and each wrong depth, what follows: twelve clauses, each with a consequence stated in the same register as the prescription. The condition there is not a date and not a state of a party but a pairing of the two, the season with the depth; and the failure is located neither in the time nor in either party but in the mismatch, which the chapter names in so many words when it says that one who needles without knowing the seasons of the channels turns the with-the-grain into the against-the-grain. A transmission can therefore bind to a calendar, state its consequence, and put the failure in a relation rather than in a place."@en ;
    skos:note "The opposition, and it is the reason this protocol stands at cases-with-deviation rather than at cases-without. The Key of Solomon binds its operations to days and hours — a whole chapter of the first book is given to them — and it is also the transmission in this holding that says at the greatest length where a failure lies. If having performed an experiment with due observance of days, hours and requisite solemnities thou shalt find it unsuccessful, it says, thou must assuredly have failed in some matter; and it names four causes for a spirit that does not come, the operator's fault, a troubled and windy sky, a rival exorcist elsewhere in the world, and the pure nature of the spirits themselves. Not one of the four is the time. A transmission that binds to the calendar as firmly as any in the population, and enumerates the accounts available for a session that produced nothing, leaves the calendar off the list entirely."@en ;
    skos:note "Why that is an opposition and not a refutation, stated exactly. The clause examined says that where such a transmission states a consequence for acting outside the window, it locates the failure in the time. The Key of Solomon states no consequence for acting outside the window; it states a consequence for acting inside it and getting nothing. The antecedent is therefore not met and the clause survives. What the case shows is how narrow the clause is: it describes the one kind of failure these transmissions almost never discuss, and it is silent about the kind they discuss constantly. Eleven of the fifteen say nothing about the window, and at least one of those eleven has a full apparatus for assigning blame that does not mention it."@en ;
    skos:note "The correction this protocol recommends, written out here and not carried back into the node, because a claim is corrected where it is examined. The statement should be split in two, and the two halves have very different standing. First: transmissions that bind an operation to a calendar state the condition as a date and not as a state of a party — carried by every one of the fifteen cases, with no exception found, and strong. Second: where such a transmission states a consequence for acting outside the window, it locates the failure in the time — carried by five and unmet by ten, and true of a class so small that it should be stated as what it is, an observation about five transmissions, rather than as a regularity. A third statement is now available and the holding does not have it: a transmission that binds an operation to a calendar and elsewhere enumerates the causes of a failed session does not put the calendar among them."@en ;
    skos:note "Five cases were considered and set outside the population, and they are recorded because the boundary is where this protocol could be wrong. Two are dates attached to something other than admissibility: the twentieth chapter of the Suwen reads the hour of a patient's death off the pulse by way of a season, which dates an outcome and not a permission; and the rubric of the hundred and first chapter of the Book of the Dead is tied to the day of the burial, which is an event and not a calendar. One is bound to a place and not a time, the prohibition on sewing new caribou skin on the island of Oqshoriaq. And two are the releases the claim already records at its own node: the fire phases that use no hour, and the man who guards the One and need not divine the day. To these the present run adds a third release, the route of wind and sun in the rasayana lesson, which is entered at the node rather than here."@en ;
    skos:note "One case belongs to neither side and is the sharpest thing in the neighbourhood. The first-century treatise against the choosing of days argues that King Wu won his battle on a jiazi day and the tyrant lost his on the same jiazi day, the two armies facing one another with their standards in sight: one day and two fates. That is not a transmission of the kind the claim describes but a critic of them, and it is held at its own dispute node. It is named here because a protocol on this claim should say where the discriminating argument is, and it is not inside any of the fifteen."@en ;
    skos:note "What this protocol does not establish. The population is this holding, and the holding was assembled by runs that found stated conditions interesting, so a transmission that binds to a calendar without saying so in a quotable sentence is exactly the kind a compiler passes over. The state is set at cases-with-deviation and not higher for that reason. The ninth witness added on the same day is not counted among the eight confirming cases above, because a witness a run enters is not a case the run's own examination found."@en .
